It's pretty close between Ranger and Monk. IMO the only thing that
made Tourist tough was the early low hp, but IMO getting a large dog
to minetown with the magic mapping scrolls and healing potions isn't
too tough. Start using a mattock as soon as you can until you can get
a decent artifact to #twoweapon with.

Ranger on the other hand is just a huge pain in the ass to deal with
arrows all the time, I ended up wishing for Magicbane and using that
most of the game, since Rangers can advance in dagger and the curse
absorption is very nice. My damage output sucked though and I never
did get past skilled in bow.

I did conducts with my Monk which obviously made it tougher (I think
weaponless was more of a challenge than vegan) and I did bodyarmorless
too (even though its not a tracked conduct). Was fortunate to get an
"oR pretty early (I don't recall if it was sokoban, it may have been
on the floor actually) and ascended wearing it. Still, though,
protection plus maxed armor (used a throne wish that I didn't need on
a spellbook of drain life - R was genocided, had already written My
Diary many times with another marker that I didn't need). In
retrospect a spellbook of finger of death may have been more useful
than maxing my HoB but anyway, not too bad.

The other race that I found challenging was Priest, believe it or not.
Less so than Monk and Ranger but still tough. A caster class that
can't improve on hunger consumption and starts with really crappy
physical stats, armor, potentially useless spells and not able to
advance in edged weapons or attack spells is not made up by having
intrinsic buc-id. While priests can quite badass late game and
stacking potions and scrolls in gehennom without an altar is quite
nice, the early game is quite difficult, much moreso than Tourist in
my opinion.

Honorable mention goes to knight and caveman (quest nemeses that cast
destroy armor, and its frustrating to have to get some other MR source
prior to getting their quest artifacts that confer it. My knight game
I fought Ixoth without it and lost my speed boots :-/) and then
samurai (bisection risk & Ashikaga gets a lot of attacks). But even so
they aren't even close to the above 3. Also any class that can't get
basic in divination and has low pw gain makes gehennom a lot slower
but not really any more difficult.

Arc is my favorite class, good #twoweapon ability, good saber, starts
with a pickaxe and can use a mattock in the early midgame, one of the
best normal weapons, also attack & divination spells, lawfuls can get
gifted grayswandir, starting tinning kit, touchstone makes it easy to
prot racket, and also have the best lawful quest artifact.

